## 6. World Model Architecture

```mermaid
flowchart LR
	A[Input sequence<br/>(T, 32)] --> B[Conv1D<br/>64 filters, k=3]
	B --> C[Batch normalization]
	C --> D[Conv1D<br/>128 filters, k=3]
	D --> E[Batch normalization]
	E --> F[Bidirectional LSTM<br/>128 units]
	F --> G[Dropout 0.3]
	G --> H[Shared latent<br/>Dense 128 + ReLU]
	H --> I[Batch normalization]
	I --> J[State head<br/>32 linear values]
	I --> K[Attack head<br/>16-class softmax]
	I --> L[Tactic head<br/>7-class softmax]
```

### Why the model is multi-task

The three heads describe different views of the same latent network state:

- **State head:** predicts what the next traffic feature vector may look like.
- **Attack head:** predicts a specific attack signature when the training labels support it.
- **Tactic head:** predicts the broader attack-stage progression used for risk scoring.

This separation lets the dashboard show a high-level operational risk even when a precise attack signature is uncertain.

### Tactic risk calculation

The inference layer computes an expected risk from tactic probabilities:

$$
R_t = \min\left(1, \sum_i P(\mathrm{tactic}_i \mid X_t) \times W_i\right)
$$

Current tactic weights are:

| Tactic | Weight |
| --- | ---: |
| None | 0.00 |
| Reconnaissance | 0.20 |
| Initial Access | 0.50 |
| Lateral Movement | 0.85 |
| Command and Control | 0.90 |
| Impact | 0.80 |
| Exfiltration | 1.00 |

These weights are prioritization policy, not probabilities and not a formal severity standard.

